card.statement:
(Kostant-Sugiura's theorem <<cite:Kos>>, <<cite:Si>>, see also <<cite:OV>>, Section 4.7]) A subspace $\mathfrak{b}_{\mathbb{R}}$ of $\mathfrak{a}_{\mathbb{R}}$ is a vector part of a standard Cartan subalgebra $\mathfrak{s}_{\mathbb{R}}$ if and only if

\begin{align*}
\mathfrak{b}_{\mathbb{R}} = \{x\in \mathfrak{a}_{\mathbb{R}}|\ \alpha(x) = 0 {\rm \ for \ all \ }\alpha\in \Gamma\}
\end{align*}

where $\Gamma$ is a strongly orthogonal $\theta$-system.
Moreover, if $\mathfrak{b}'_{\mathbb{R}}$ is the vector part of another Cartan subalgebras $\mathfrak{s}'_{\mathbb{R}}$ of $\mathfrak{g}_{\mathbb{R}}$ defined by the strongly orthogonal $\theta$-system
$\Omega$, then $\mathfrak{s}_{\mathbb{R}}$ and $\mathfrak{s}'_{\mathbb{R}}$ are conjugate if and only if
there is an element $w$ in the Weyl group $\mathcal{W}$ that commutes with $\theta$ and $w\Omega = \Gamma$.
